{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling: J4's instance records the hold's own lane as its task reference until platform:task@1 is supplied\n\nDecision. For J4's first hosted probe and for J4 acceptance, the task field of the step instance names the lane scope that owns the hold (its reference with its incarnation). No native task is claimed. The ruling states a stand-in, and the records say so.\n\nWhy this field and nothing more. The authority note, section 5.7, defines the instance record's value as the task scope's reference, the scope of the hosted agent's runtime that reads the hold's epoch (section 5.9) and whose signer provisions tokens with the holder's own member key (the evidence table, \"Provision or renew a hold's write token\"). That scope is platform:task@1, which no package supplies: the directory's open-task row is built and its child scope is not (directory.ts:293-315), and docs/scopes.md says so twice. In J4 there is no hosted runtime between the holder and the lane: the holder's own key signs the staging, fork and token steps, and the lane judges them. The lane is therefore the scope that does the task's work in J4, and it is a real scope with a supported genesis and read path.\n\nWhat reads the field. hold.ts reads task only in taskOf (line 303) for export-license and export-settled (lines 317-352, 412, 450), the export path, which is outside J4's adopted boundary (adb1a6ed: the fork and head outcome rules only). The fork, token and head rules J4 adds read hold, instance and epoch, never task. So the stand-in changes no judge answer in J4's scope.\n\nLimits to state, in the J4 acceptance text and in docs/hosts.md's J4 row.\n1. The instance's task value is the hold's lane; it is a stand-in, not a task scope.\n2. No rule of J4 reads it. The export rules do, and exports are not part of J4; an export under this stand-in would name the lane as its source task, and that path is not exercised.\n3. When platform:task@1 is supplied, the step instance names the real task in a new instance, and the step moves the stand-in instance to past (section 5.7). No record is rewritten.\n4. The native test graph.instance() retags a lane as task for the same reason; this ruling adopts the interpretation on its own grounds, and the fixture is not its authority.\n\nNot adopted: an external task identity, a task engine, or any hosted-agent lifecycle work in J4. A deliberate stage refusal without an instance is not the first observation; the first hosted observation under adb1a6ed is a stage on an instance that names the lane.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}